/* datachannelxt.map - the linker version script for the shipped library.
 *
 * WHY: datachannelxt statically links libdatachannel, libjuice, usrsctp and
 * plog, and a static archive's symbols inherit the shared library's default
 * (public) visibility. The shipped .so therefore used to export about 6000
 * dynamic symbols (35 juice_ ones, 479 usrsctp_ and sctp_ ones, plus the C++
 * runtime bits from -static-libstdc++) alongside the 32 dcx_ entry points that
 * are the
 * actual ABI. Another extension in the same engine process bundling its own
 * copy of any of those libraries is then an interposition hazard: whichever
 * definition the loader resolved first would win for both. The engine's dlopen
 * is typically RTLD_LOCAL, which makes this latent rather than live, but a
 * shipped artifact should not depend on the host's dlopen flags for its
 * correctness.
 *
 * -fvisibility=hidden on our own target cannot fix it (the dependencies are
 * built by their own CMake targets, out of reach of our flags); a version
 * script filters at LINK time, so it does.
 *
 * dcx::test::* is exported ON PURPOSE. The smoke test links the REAL shared
 * library so that every call it makes goes through the real export surface -
 * testing a differently-linked copy would prove less - and these helpers
 * (live_peer_count, queue_depth, force_throw, ...) are how it inspects state
 * the C ABI deliberately does not expose. They are C++-mangled and
 * unreachable from the FFI, and the sibling torrentxt ships its btx::test::*
 * helpers for the same reason.
 *
 * GNU ld / lld only (Linux). The Windows lane controls exports with a .def
 * file and macOS uses default visibility, so CMakeLists applies this only
 * where the linker supports it.
 */
{
    global:
        dcx_*;
        _ZN3dcx4test*;   /* dcx::test::* - see above */
    local:
        *;
};
